 "Ms Lan has become something of a familiar face at the ticket counter of the Greenfield Box Office, the "
 "friendly little booth in the town square where Ms Wickmoor works every day selling tickets to almost "
 "everything worth seeing around town. Ms Wickmoor handles tickets for the cinema down the street, the "
 "concert hall that hosts everything from quiet piano recitals to loud rock shows, the small but lovely "
 "art museum, the aquarium with its popular dolphin show, and the theme park just outside town with its "
 "roller coasters and water rides. Ms Lan comes in for all sorts of reasons -- sometimes she wants two "
 "tickets for a Friday night movie with her husband, sometimes she's buying four tickets for her "
 "children's favorite animated film, and sometimes she's trying to work out whether the museum's student "
 "discount applies to her teenage daughter. Many of their conversations start with a simple question "
 "about what time a show begins or whether tickets are still available for a particular evening, since "
 "popular shows tend to sell out quickly and Ms Lan has learned it rarely hurts to ask early. Plenty of "
 "their exchanges are about seating, because Ms Lan likes a good view of the screen or the stage without "
 "sitting too close to the speakers, and plenty more are about price, since she always appreciates "
 "knowing exactly what a group discount or a student rate will save her before she commits to buying. "
 "They also work through the smaller practical details that come with planning an outing -- confirming "
 "the age limit on a late-night horror film, asking how long the intermission lasts at a long concert, "
 "checking whether a wheelchair-accessible seat is still open near the front, and finding out what "
 "happens to a ticket when a show gets cancelled or pushed back a week because of bad weather. None of "
 "these exchanges are especially dramatic, and that's exactly the point. This is what everyday "
 "conversation about entertainment and events sounds like between a patient box-office clerk and one of "
 "her most regular customers, ticket after ticket. On any given week they might be talking about a "
 "sold-out jazz festival downtown, a special VIP package for an upcoming concert, or whether it's worth "
 "buying tickets for the theme park online in advance instead of waiting in line at the gate, and "
 "somehow the conversation always stays easy and pleasant. Ms Lan has learned that it always pays to ask "
 "one more question before walking away from the counter, whether that means double-checking a showtime, "
 "confirming a refund policy, or understanding exactly what a VIP ticket includes, and Ms Wickmoor has "
 "learned that a few extra minutes spent explaining things clearly almost always saves a confused phone "
 "call later that same day."
